<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1453">1453</a>–<a href="/years/1515">1515</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as El Gran Capitán, the Great Captain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonzalo Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, known as the Great Captain, was a Castilian general who fought in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and then commanded Spanish armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, arquebusiers and fortified positions, he defeated the French at Cerignola and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 1503, winning the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>, and served as its viceroy until 1507. His methods shaped the later Spanish infantry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba.jpg" alt="Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo Carrió · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_gran_capit%C3%A1n_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Ferdinand II the Crown joined Castile in the conquest of Granada, completed in 1492, and <b>Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</b> won Naples back from France at Cerignola and the Garigliano in 1503.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Isabella I and Ferdinand II fought the Granada War of 1482–1492 with large armies, siege artillery and an efficient supply system, experience that <b>Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</b> carried into the Italian Wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ferdinand II of Aragon agreed in 1500 to partition the kingdom, then fell out, and the Spanish commander <b>Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</b>, the 'Great Captain', won the Battle of Cerignola, one of the first battles decided largely by handguns, and the Battle of the Garigliano, both in 1503.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish military of the sixteenth century was the most formidable in Europe, shaped by the long warfare of the Reconquista, the tactical lessons of the Italian Wars under commanders such as <b>Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</b>, 'the Great Captain', and the combination of firearms with disciplined formation warfare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After weeks of stalemate on the river Garigliano, <b>Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</b>'s Spanish army crossed by surprise and routed the French under Ludovico II of Saluzzo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-29">29 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat led to <b>Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</b>'s creation of the tercios.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
